Baptiste,

I've looked at this in a bit more detail, and it's possible that it behaves differently depending on the output device. Or, at least, the results are different.

If you run the code below interactively, you can see the first plot print in the display window, then the second, then the align.plots() call realigns the plots and they redisplay. In my case it looks like the top (first) plot moves to the right a few millimetres. Of course, that depends on the sizes of the plots.

If you run the same data in a program that prints to a PDF, it looks exactly like a second copy of the top plot has overprinted the first copy, i.e. a bit of the Y-axis label is visible at the left of the Y-axis label for the realigned plot.

Is there a way of avoiding that?

regards - David

