Hi:

It sounds as though the reorder() function might be useful in this context. For example, in Figure 11.2 of the Lattice book, state.name <http://state.name> is reordered by Frost value in order to get the 'increasing response' appearance in each panel. In this case, however, it seems that you want to reorder factor levels by some (summary) measure and then apply that reordering to the factor levels in order to influence panel placement order with facet_wrap() or facet_grid(). You can do the calculations and reordering before plotting by creating a new factor with level order determined by reorder() for each desired factor and then use the reordered factors in the ggplot. I don't think there is a facility within the faceting functions themselves to do this on the fly within a ggplot() call. I'm sure that Hadley or another ggplot2 guru will chime in if I'm in error on this point.

BTW, that section of the xyplot help page also says:
"Although they can be supplied in high level function calls directly, it is more typical to use perm.cond and index.cond to update an existing "trellis" object, thus allowing it to be displayed in a different arrangement without re-calculating the data subsets that go into each panel. In the update method, both can be set to NULL, which reverts these back to their defaults."

...which rather suggests to me that the ordering can be determined by external means before being applied to the graphic object.

HTH,
Dennis

